   "context_tokens": 32,
   "fp16": {
    "text": " Speculative decoding is a technique used in language models, particularly in the context of large language models (LLMs), to improve inference speed without altering the output. Here's how it works and why it achieves this:\n\n1. **Parallel Processing**: In traditional decoding, each token is generated sequentially, one after another. Speculative decoding, on the other hand, allows for parallel processing of multiple tokens. This means that while the model is generating one token, it can start working on the next few tokens simultaneously. This parallelism significantly reduces the overall time required for inference.\n\n2. **Efficient Use of Resources**: By processing multiple tokens at once, speculative decoding makes more efficient use of computational resources. It can take advantage of hardware accelerators like GPUs or TPUs more effectively, which are designed to handle parallel computations.\n\n3. **No Change in Output Quality**: Despite the speedup, speculative decoding does not affect the quality of the generated text. The model",
