   "bitnest": {
    "text": " Speculative decoding is a technique used in language models, particularly in the context of large language models (LLMs), to improve inference speed without altering the output. Here's how it works and why it achieves this:\n\n1. **Parallel Processing**: LLMs are often designed to process text in parallel, which means they can generate multiple tokens simultaneously. Speculative decoding takes advantage of this by predicting several possible next tokens at once, rather than generating them sequentially.\n\n2. **Reduced Computation**: By predicting multiple tokens at once, speculative decoding reduces the number of computations needed to generate the final output. This is because the model doesn't have to wait for each token to be generated before moving on to the next one.\n\n3. **Efficient Use of Resources**: Speculative decoding allows the model to use its computational resources more efficiently. Instead of spending time on generating one token at a time, it can generate several tokens in parallel, which can significantly speed",
